// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 1 (memory-audit-4e
// whole-branch review, probe P5): an element of one global container read
// into another global. Before the fix, `cl_free_globals` had no runtime-
// aliasing analysis, so it always froze both `p5Gl` (element-freeing its
// one stored handle) and `p5GSaved` (freeing the very same handle again):
// SIGSEGV. Now the globals escape pre-pass reuses the existing ExIndex-read
// rule (already applied to locals/window vars): reading `p5Gl[0]`
// disqualifies `p5Gl` (it can hand back an element the container still
// owns), and rule 4 disqualifies `p5GSaved` (assigned from a non-fresh
// ExIndex read). Neither is freed. Expected strict-mode leak count: 6 (see
// ../p5_global_elem_alias.leaks) -- `p5Gl`'s own list (struct box +
// backing-array handle, 2 blocks) plus the one `"x"+"y"` text handle it
// holds (2 blocks, shared with `p5GSaved`, counted once) plus `p5GSaved`'s
// own original empty-text default handle (2 blocks), orphaned when the
// index-read store overwrites it without freeing the old value first --
// same orphan-on-overwrite class as P2's `p2G2`, independent of this fix.

var p5Gl: list of text
var p5GSaved: text

func runP5GlobalElemAlias() {
    p5Gl.push("x" + "y")
    p5GSaved = p5Gl[0]
    alert("p5 done")
}
